# LF Studio settings. Copy to ~/.config/lf-studio/env (install/90-env.sh writes one for you).
# Read by bin/lf-studio, install/*.sh and bench/*.sh. One KEY=VALUE per line; shell quoting, $VAR and ~
# expand; lines are evaluated in order, so later lines can use earlier keys, and a later line for the same key
# overrides an earlier one. A variable already set in the environment wins over this file: spark-switchboard
# (https://github.com/slogonomo/spark-switchboard) passes LFSTUDIO_PORT and LFSTUDIO_HOST that way. A $VAR that is not
# set stops the scripts with this file's name and line.
# Until 0.6 a file (or environment) with the names up to 0.4 works too: SOLH3_PORT for LFSTUDIO_PORT, and so on, with
# one warning; the new name wins. So does ~/.config/sol-h3-studio/env while ~/.config/lf-studio/env does not exist,
# and ~/.local/state/sol-h3-studio while it has the clip history (jobs.db) and ~/.local/state/lf-studio does not.

# ---- the machine ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# spark (the default when unset) = NVIDIA's Sol-H3-Spark package on one DGX Spark. rtx5090 = Sol-H3-RTX5090 on a
# workstation with one GeForce RTX 5090 (x86_64; docs/RTX5090.md, docs/INSTALL.md "RTX 5090"). It picks the package
# in LFSTUDIO_SANA, the Sana commit and patch (patches/BASE_COMMIT and lf-studio.patch, or BASE_COMMIT_RTX5090 and
# lf-studio-rtx5090.patch), the install variants (no Docker image and Python from uv on the RTX 5090), the
# launcher's checks and memory floors, and the machine the page names. Any other value is refused, and so is a
# value that does not match the patched package. install/00-check.sh and 90-env.sh detect it (GB10 = spark,
# RTX 5090 = rtx5090) and refuse when the machine looks like an RTX 5090 but it was never set; 90-env.sh writes the
# value the install used here. Left commented out so that a copy of this file does not set it for you.
# LFSTUDIO_BACKEND=spark

# ---- where things are -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base directory for everything the install scripts create (checkout, envs, sources, runtime, manifests).
LFSTUDIO_HOME="$HOME/sol-h3"
# The patched NVlabs/Sana checkout (branch sol-engine + patches/lf-studio.patch). The Sol-H3-Spark package
# is $LFSTUDIO_SANA/models/minimax_h3/Sol-H3-Spark (with LFSTUDIO_BACKEND=rtx5090: patches/lf-studio-rtx5090.patch,
# and $LFSTUDIO_SANA/models/minimax_h3/Sol-H3-RTX5090).
LFSTUDIO_SANA="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/Sana"
# Model weights (download_checkpoints.py --output-dir). Point it at an existing checkpoints directory to reuse it.
LFSTUDIO_CHECKPOINTS="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/checkpoints"
# Python that runs the web server (stdlib only). The Stage1 venv's python is used by default.
LFSTUDIO_PYTHON="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/envs/stage1/bin/python"
# Path manifests written by prepare.py (install/70-manifests.sh); {task} = t2va, fl2va or ref2va.
LFSTUDIO_PATHS="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/paths-{task}.json"
# Install scripts only: the modes to download weights for (20) and write manifests for (70). Set it here, before
# step 20, if you want fewer than all three (for example "t2va"); the studio offers only modes with a manifest.
LFSTUDIO_TASKS="t2va fl2va ref2va"
# Install scripts only, RTX 5090 only: a copy of a DGX Spark's fixed-prompt.pt. NVIDIA's cache builder needs an
# SM121 GPU, so install/80-prompt-cache.sh checks this file with the package's recipe check and copies it to
# $LFSTUDIO_HOME/fixed-prompt.pt instead of building one.
# LFSTUDIO_PROMPT_CACHE_FROM=
# Install scripts only, RTX 5090 only: where uv puts its CPython 3.12.15 and 3.13.16 (install/05-python.sh).
# UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_DIR="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/python"
# Install scripts only, RTX 5090 only: pip's cache (~6.3 GB of wheels after steps 40 and 50) and uv's (a few KB),
# kept under $LFSTUDIO_HOME so that a rerun reuses them. On a DGX Spark pip uses its default, ~/.cache/pip.
# PIP_CACHE_DIR="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/cache/pip"
# UV_CACHE_DIR="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/cache/uv"

# ---- read by NVIDIA's Sol-H3-Spark runtime (qwen_python.sh) -------------------------------------------------------
# Dedicated, writable, absolute directory. The Qwen container mounts it read-write; uploads, session outputs and
# the persistent compile cache ($SOL_H3_SPARK_RUNTIME_ROOT/cache/compile) live here.
SOL_H3_SPARK_RUNTIME_ROOT="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/runtime"
# DGX Spark only (the RTX 5090 runs the Qwen encoder natively, without Docker; there these two are not used):
# Docker image of the Qwen prompt encoder (install/60-qwen-image.sh builds it).
SOL_H3_SPARK_QWEN_IMAGE=sol-h3-spark-qwen
# Extra read-only mounts for the Qwen container: the directory holding the NVFP4 Qwen checkpoint, and ComfyUI.
SOL_H3_SPARK_QWEN_WEIGHTS_ROOT="$LFSTUDIO_CHECKPOINTS/Comfy-MiniMax-H3"
SOL_H3_SPARK_COMFY_ROOT="$LFSTUDIO_HOME/sources/ComfyUI"

# ---- web UI -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 127.0.0.1 = this machine only (use NVIDIA Sync or an SSH tunnel from your laptop). 0.0.0.0 = the whole LAN;
# there is no login, so only do that on a network you trust.
LFSTUDIO_HOST=127.0.0.1
LFSTUDIO_PORT=8190
# Host names the studio answers to besides IP addresses, localhost, names without a dot (Tailscale MagicDNS short
# names) and names ending in .ts.net, .local, .lan, .home, .home.arpa or .internal: a comma list, ports ignored,
# for example a domain of your own that points at the studio. A request for any other name gets 421, so a web page
# can't reach the studio through DNS rebinding. * = any name (the check is off).
LFSTUDIO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# jobs.db (clip history, every clip), load-times.json, server.log and launcher.log. The default, left commented out
# until 0.6: a value set here turns off the use of ~/.local/state/sol-h3-studio (the top of this file).
# LFSTUDIO_STATE_DIR="$HOME/.local/state/lf-studio"
# Optional: also copy every finished clip (and its poster) here, e.g. a shared folder. Empty = off. Deleting a
# clip in the studio moves this copy to the trash as well.
LFSTUDIO_PUBLISH_DIR=
# 1 = at start, warm up the mode/format/length of the last finished clip; 0 = load models on the first job.
# A clip with the same settings submitted early in the warm-up, while that is faster, takes it over
# (docs/PERFORMANCE.md).
LFSTUDIO_PRELOAD=1
# 1 = a clip in the loaded mode but with another format or length keeps the models loaded: the running session
# changes its shape, and the first clip at a new shape compiles for it. 0 = close and load the models again for any
# change of mode, format or length (before 0.4). A change of mode always reloads.
LFSTUDIO_RESHAPE=1
# Days a deleted clip stays in $SOL_H3_SPARK_RUNTIME_ROOT/trash (Undo, or recovery by hand) before it is removed
# for good, with its history entry. Checked at start and once a day.
LFSTUDIO_TRASH_DAYS=7
# Clips that may wait in the queue (the running one does not count). A further submit, from the page or a script,
# is refused with 429 "the queue is full" until one has started.
LFSTUDIO_MAX_QUEUED=20

# ---- agents and scripts (bin/lf-cli, docs/API.md) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The studio's API for programs: JSON submits and GET /api/jobs/<id>[/video], which bin/lf-cli, its MCP server and
# scripts use. 1 (the default, as in 0.4) = on. 0 = the page only: those answer 403 "the API for programs is off on
# this studio (LFSTUDIO_API=0)", and so does a form submit from bin/lf-cli, which then says so; the page works as
# before. Off is not access control: anyone who reaches the studio can still send the page's own form from a script
# (docs/API.md, "Turning the API off"). Any other value is refused at start. Left unset here so that a later
# release that turns it off by default reaches copies of this file; set it to keep it on.
# LFSTUDIO_API=1
# bin/lf-studio generate|wait|status|cancel|fetch|guide|mcp runs the client with this file's settings. It finds the
# studio at LFSTUDIO_HOST and LFSTUDIO_PORT (0.0.0.0 counts as 127.0.0.1; under spark-switchboard, LFSTUDIO_PORT here is
# switchboard's public port, which is the right one), unless LFSTUDIO_URL names another address. On other machines, set
# LFSTUDIO_URL in the environment of bin/lf-cli or of its MCP server (with LFSTUDIO_OUT_DIR, LFSTUDIO_MCP_WAIT_S and the
# other MCP settings in docs/API.md); this file is not read there.
# LFSTUDIO_URL=http://<spark-host>:8190

# ---- launcher -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Refuse to start below this much MemAvailable (GiB). 0 = no check. Default 110 on the DGX Spark: the pipeline
# needs nearly the whole box (lowest MemAvailable measured during a 5 s clip: ~10-12 GiB of ~121, a session's first
# clip included; docs/PERFORMANCE.md, Memory). Default 42 on the RTX 5090: every mode ran in a 48 GiB memory limit
# (43.3 GiB peak; docs/RTX5090.md).
# LFSTUDIO_MIN_FREE_GIB=110
# RTX 5090 only: refuse to start below this much free GPU memory (GiB, nvidia-smi memory.free); the desktop and
# other apps share the card's 32 GB. 0 = no check. Default 25: every mode needed at most 24.8 GiB
# (docs/RTX5090.md). On the
# DGX Spark the GPU uses the RAM checked above, and this is not read.
# LFSTUDIO_MIN_FREE_VRAM_GIB=25
# Stop the app when MemAvailable stays below this (GiB) for 3 s, so the box does not hang. 0 = no guard.
LFSTUDIO_GUARD_GIB=4
# Seconds the server may take to close its pipeline on stop before it is killed (it usually needs ~40 s). Keep it
# below a manager's own limit: the spark-switchboard service file passes 75 against switchboard's 90 s.
LFSTUDIO_STOP_WAIT_S=90
# Optional pidfile with the launcher's pid (spark-switchboard uses it to find the launcher after a restart).
LFSTUDIO_PIDFILE=
# Server output (default $LFSTUDIO_STATE_DIR/server.log).
# LFSTUDIO_LOG=

# ---- patch switches (defaults shown; see docs/PATCH.md) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Persistent Inductor/Triton caches. Default $SOL_H3_SPARK_RUNTIME_ROOT/cache/compile; "session" = upstream's
# per-session caches (recompiles on every start).
# SOL_H3_COMPILE_CACHE=
# SPARK_FAST_SAFETENSORS=1         # 0 = upstream safetensors loading
# SPARK_DIRECT_IO=1                # 0 = no O_DIRECT reads (mmap + copy)
# SPARK_DIRECT_AHEAD=1             # 0 = no read-ahead of the next O_DIRECT window
# SOL_H3_BSA_CHECK_CALLS=8         # 200 = upstream's full cuDNN BSA self-check on the first request
# SPARK_AUTOTUNE_CACHE_FIX=1       # 0 = upstream Inductor autotune-cache behaviour (torch 2.13)
# SPARK_DEBUG_AUTOTUNE=0           # 1 = print autotune-cache fallback hits, kernels tuned at run time, stored results
# SOL_H3_VAE_ON_DEVICE=1           # 0 = build the VAEs on the CPU as upstream
# SOL_H3_AUDIO_VAE_PREFETCH=1      # 0 = load the audio VAE when first needed

# ---- RTX 5090 memory (LFSTUDIO_BACKEND=rtx5090 only; docs/RTX5090.md) ------------------------------------------------
# Put both directories on a disk without compression or copy-on-write (btrfs: chattr +C; the launcher does that for
# directories it creates). Default: next to $LFSTUDIO_CHECKPOINTS.
# SOL_H3_FILEBACKED_DIR=<checkpoints>/../scratch      # H3's pages while it is built (0 = anonymous RAM, as upstream:
#                                                     # needs ~73 GB); files are deleted as they are made, no space kept
# SOL_H3_LTX_BLOCKS=<checkpoints>/../derived/ltx-fused-blocks   # LTX with its LoRA fused once (~37 GB, built by the
#                                                     # first session in about a minute; 0 = upstream: all 40 GB pinned)
# SOL_H3_LTX_RESIDENT_BLOCKS=auto  # LTX blocks kept in RAM; the others are read from disk each step. auto = what fits
# SOL_H3_LTX_RAM_RESERVE_GIB=12    #   in the available RAM minus this reserve (for Qwen, page cache, compile workers)
# SOL_H3_LTX_READ_AHEAD=3          # LTX blocks read ahead from disk
# SOL_H3_LTX_VERIFY=0              # 1 = check every block read against its SHA-256 (slow; for testing)
# SOL_H3_STAGE1_PIN=1              # 0 = keep H3's blocks in pageable RAM, as upstream
# SOL_H3_QWEN_FAST_LOAD=1          # 0 = upstream's one-slot O_DIRECT Qwen loader (same weights, ~3x slower)
# SOL_H3_STAGE1_SNAPSHOTS=<checkpoints>/../derived/stage1-snapshots   # H3 as built, saved once per mode (~23 GB each)
#                                                     # and restored in ~5 s instead of built in ~155 s (0 = build
#                                                     # every session). Tied to the checkpoint, LoRA, code and GPU.
# SOL_H3_STAGE1_SNAPSHOTS_KEEP=1   # snapshots kept per mode (the launcher's default; the patch's own default is 2)
# SOL_H3_STAGE1_SNAPSHOT_VERIFY=1  # 0 = skip the SHA-256 check of a snapshot's blocks when restoring
# SOL_H3_STAGE1_READ_AHEAD=1       # 0 = no read-ahead of the next H3 block while it is built
# SOL_H3_KEEP_VAE_DECODER=0        # 1 = keep the H3 video VAE's decoder in fl2va/ref2va (unused; 9.7 GB of RAM)
# SOL_H3_MALLOC_TRIM=1             # 0 = workers keep freed memory (the idle footprint then grows ~1 GB per clip)
# SOL_H3_STAGE2_AUDIO=frozen       # joint = upstream: Stage 2 re-noises the audio and redraws the lips to its own copy
# SOL_H3_DYNAMIC_TEXT_LENGTH=1     # 0 = Stage 1's body graphs compile per prompt length (as upstream), not per session
# How the prompt encoder tokenizes MiniMax's tags. minimax (default since 0.4) = as MiniMax's own tokenizer: <d>, </d>,
# <|cutoff|> and 4 more are single tokens, as in H3's training. plain = as the pinned ComfyUI and studio 0.3 did: <d>
# and </d> become ordinary text tokens. Prompts without these tags encode the same in both. A dialogue clip made with
# 0.3 reproduces from its seed only with plain. minimax needs the 0.4 patch: after a git pull, run install/10-sana.sh.
# SOL_H3_PROMPT_TOKENS=minimax
# SOL_H3_RESIDENT_QWEN_MAX_FRAMES=241   # longer clips load the prompt encoder per request
# SOL_H3_STATIC_SHAPES=1           # 0 = upstream torch.compile(dynamic=None): after a size change a graph may get
#                                  # dynamic sizes, so a session's output can depend on what it ran before
# SOL_H3_RESIDENT_QWEN_EARLY=1     # 0 = load the resident prompt encoder after the first clip, not during its refine
# SOL_H3_RESIDENT_QWEN_EARLY_MIN_GIB=     # GiB free needed to load it during that refine, or right after a change
#                                  # back to 10 s or less (empty: 32 up to 5 s, 34 above). Below it, it loads after
#                                  # the first clip; after a change, the next clip loads its own encoder first
# infer.py only. The web server removes these two from its environment at start (it sets format and length per
# job), so they affect only infer.py runs without --orientation / --seconds.
# SOL_H3_ORIENTATION=landscape     # infer.py default when --orientation is not given
# SOL_H3_SECONDS=                  # infer.py default when --seconds is not given (empty = frozen 5 s route)
# MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=0           # set for the workers unless you set it yourself

# ---- test hooks (tests only; leave unset) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LFSTUDIO_SERVER=...              # run this script instead of webui/server.py
# LFSTUDIO_TEST_SKIP_DOCKER=1      # skip the docker checks
# LFSTUDIO_CONTAINER_PREFIX=...    # container name prefix the launcher cleans up (default sol-h3-qwen-)
